# Slop Factory — Game Contract

You are creating a small standalone browser game for the Slop Factory gallery. Your output is published unedited.

## Working directory

You are running in an isolated working directory. You may read and write files here. You will be given the game concept in the prompt itself. Do **not** modify this `CONVENTIONS.md` file.

## Required output files

Produce both of these files in the working directory:

1. **`index.html`** — a single self-contained HTML page that is the game.
2. **`meta.json`** — metadata describing the game, used by the gallery.

You may write scratch files for your own use; only the two above are published.

## `meta.json` schema

```json
{
  "title": "Neon Dodger",
  "description": "Dodge falling neon blocks in a tiny ship. Stay alive as long as you can.",
  "controls": ["Arrow keys to move", "Space to fire"],
  "tags": ["arcade", "dodge"]
}
```

Rules:
- `title` — string, 3–80 characters, matches the game's display name.
- `description` — string, 20–400 characters, one short paragraph.
- `controls` — non-empty array of short strings, one per control.
- `tags` — optional array of lowercase keywords.

## `index.html` requirements

### Self-contained
- All CSS goes in a single `<style>` block in `<head>`.
- All JS goes in a single `<script>` block at the end of `<body>`.
- **No** external resources: no `<script src="https://...">`, no `<link href="https://...">`, no `<img src="https://...">`, no CDN imports.
- **No** network calls at runtime: no `fetch`, no `XMLHttpRequest`, no `import()`, no `WebSocket`.

### Required structure (in this order)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>YOUR TITLE</title>
  <style>/* inline only */</style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="game-header">
    <h1>YOUR TITLE</h1>
    <p class="game-description">YOUR DESCRIPTION</p>
    <ul class="game-controls">
      <li>YOUR CONTROL</li>
    </ul>
  </header>
  <main>
    <canvas id="game" width="800" height="600"></canvas>
    <button id="restart" class="game-restart" type="button">Restart</button>
  </main>
  <script>/* inline only */</script>
</body>
</html>
```

- Exactly one `<canvas id="game">` (you may set width/height to other values; 800x600 is recommended).
- Exactly one `<button id="restart" class="game-restart">` immediately after the canvas. Clicking it must fully reset the game (same code path as the R key). It must be visible and clickable at all times — not only on game-over.
- The `<header>`'s `<h1>` text must match `meta.json.title` exactly.
- The `<p class="game-description">` text must match `meta.json.description` exactly.
- The `<li>` items in `<ul class="game-controls">` must match `meta.json.controls` in order.

### Behavior
- The game must start rendering immediately on page load (no "click to start" required).
- Use keyboard and/or mouse input.
- The script must not throw any uncaught exception in the first 5 seconds.
- If you use `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`, all keys must start with the prefix `slop-factory:{SLUG}:`.

### Style
- Dark background (the gallery is dark — recommend `#0a0a0f` or similar).
- High-contrast neon accents (e.g. cyan `#7df9ff`, magenta `#ff00aa`, yellow `#ffd23f`).
- The `<header>` is shown above the canvas — keep its styling minimal and let the canvas content shine.

## What makes a tiny canvas game feel good

Aim for these qualities in every game you produce:

1. **Immediate input response** — every keystroke or click produces visible action within one frame. Never make the player wait.
2. **Clear feedback** — the player always knows when something happened. Use color flashes, particles, brief screen shake, score popups, or sound on every meaningful event (hit, score, miss, death).
3. **Win/lose state** — the game has a clear end condition. On lose, draw "Game Over" + final score + restart instructions on the canvas. On win, celebrate (color burst, "You Win!" overlay).
4. **Restart without page reload** — both pressing R AND clicking the `<button id="restart">` reset the game cleanly via the same code path. Mention both in `controls` (e.g. "Press R or click Restart"). On restart you MUST `cancelAnimationFrame` any pending loop before starting a new one (otherwise loops stack on each restart and the game eventually slows to a crawl).
5. **Progressive difficulty** — start gentle so the player can learn, then ramp up. Don't dump them into chaos at t=0.
6. **No dead time** — something interesting on screen at all times. Idle background animation (stars, gradient pulse, particles) is fine.
7. **Persistent score / progress UI** — drawn on the canvas at the top corner, always visible.
8. **Audio (optional but encouraged)** — generate tones with `OscillatorNode` (no external assets). **Do not auto-start audio on page load** — browsers block this and it will throw. Initialize the `AudioContext` lazily on the first keypress or click. Wrap audio code in try/catch.

## Playable Contract — non-negotiable

These are not aspirations. A game that violates any of these is broken even if it runs without errors. After your implementation finishes, the validator loads the page in a real browser and runs an 8-second playtest with synthetic input — failures here are reported back to you and must be fixed before the game ships.

### 1. The player can lose

There must be a collision check (or other end condition) that fires `gameOver` and stops the active gameplay loop. "Game runs forever, no death" is broken. Examples that have failed in the past: collision check compared a value against itself; "spike" collision only clamped position instead of killing the player; obstacles never reached the player.

### 2. The player can score

The score must increase on a **discrete event** (a survived obstacle, a collected pickup, an enemy destroyed, a beat hit). "Score = elapsed seconds" is allowed but weak. Score must be drawn on the canvas at all times.

### 3. The first 3 seconds are survivable with no input

Define a `SAFE_RADIUS` (e.g. 200px) around the player's starting position. When spawning hazards, **reject any spawn that is within `SAFE_RADIUS` of the player AND would reach them within 3 seconds at current hazard speed**. Pre-spawning hazards on top of the player at t=0 has caused frame-1 deaths in past games. Don't do it.

### 4. The control actually changes the outcome

Whatever the player presses must, in the next frame, change a value that affects collision or score. If a control is purely cosmetic (e.g. "stretch horizontally" but the gaps are vertical), the game is fundamentally broken even if it runs. After writing, **re-read the game's concept and trace one frame**: does the input do what the concept claims?

### 5. dt-based physics, not per-frame constants

All movement and timing must scale by delta time (seconds since last frame). Per-frame constants (`x += 5` every frame) make games run 2.4× faster on a 144Hz display.

```js
let lastTime = performance.now();
function loop(now) {
  const dt = Math.min((now - lastTime) / 1000, 0.1); // seconds, clamped
  lastTime = now;
  // physics scaled by dt:
  player.x += player.vx * dt;
  enemy.y  += enemy.vy  * dt;
  spawnTimer -= dt;
  // ...
  requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
requestAnimationFrame(loop);
```

### 6. Restart fully resets AND cancels the prior loop

Both R and the Restart button must call the same reset function. That function MUST `cancelAnimationFrame(loopId)` the previous loop before scheduling a new one — otherwise loops stack on each restart and the game slows to a crawl over time.

```js
let loopId = null;
function startLoop() {
  if (loopId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(loopId);
  function tick(now) { /* ... */ loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick); }
  loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
}
```

### 7. Game-over overlay shows score and restart instructions

When the player dies, draw on the canvas: "Game Over", final score, current high score (if you persist one), and "Press R or click Restart". Do not rely on the HTML restart button alone — players need to know they can restart.

## Observable State Contract — required for playtest

In addition to the rules above, your game **must** expose its current state on `window.__game` so the automated playtest can observe what the player would observe. A game that does not expose this object is rejected.

```js
window.__game = {
  state: "menu",        // "menu" | "playing" | "gameover" — exact strings, no others
  score: 0,             // current score, number
  player: {
    x: 400,             // player x in canvas coordinates (0 = left)
    y: 300,             // player y in canvas coordinates (0 = top)
    visible: true,      // true if the player is currently rendered inside the canvas viewport
  },
  objective: "Dodge falling neon blocks. Survive as long as possible.", // 10-200 chars, one sentence
};
```

Rules:
- The object must exist from the very first frame, before any input.
- Update `state`, `score`, `player.x`, `player.y`, and `player.visible` whenever they change. The simplest approach is to update them at the end of your render/update step each frame.
- `state` must only ever be one of the three exact strings. No other values.
- `player.visible` is critical. **If your camera follows the player, the camera math must keep `player.visible` = true at all times.** A camera that lags the player off-screen is the most common failure here.
- `objective` is one short sentence written for a first-time player: "what am I trying to do?" — derivable from observing the game alone, not from reading the meta.

If your game has multiple "players" or no clear single player, expose the position of the most player-like entity (the thing the human controls).

## What the validator checks

Your output is checked programmatically. Failure feeds the error report back to you for correction.

1. Both `index.html` and `meta.json` exist.
2. `meta.json` parses as JSON and matches the schema above.
3. `index.html` parses, has exactly one `<canvas id="game">`, exactly one `<button id="restart">` after it, and the `<header>` text matches `meta.json`.
4. No external URLs in `<script src=>`, `<link href=>`, `<img src=>` (regex scan).
5. Headless Chromium loads the page; no `console.error` or `pageerror` events in 5 seconds.
6. Canvas has non-blank pixels after 3 seconds (game is actually drawing something).
7. **Observable contract is present**: `window.__game` exists with the exact shape above, from the first frame.
8. **Playtest invariants** hold during ~8 seconds of synthetic key input (arrow keys, WASD, space):
   - `state` only takes the values `"menu" | "playing" | "gameover"`.
   - `player.visible` is true for ≥95% of polled frames.
   - Either `score` changes at least once during play, or `state` reaches `"gameover"`. (A game where neither happens has no progress signal — broken even if it runs.)

## Tips

- Keep the implementation simple and robust over flashy and fragile.
- Test mentally: imagine loading the page, pressing keys, watching for 5 seconds — would anything throw?
- Use `requestAnimationFrame` for the loop. Wrap the loop body in a try/catch so a stray bug doesn't crash the whole thing.
